From 398391e12c2713d36c1a45e1cab0c03846e5594b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 08:15:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Executor: read the CCR trigger, and never guess an issue
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The executor identified its one dispatch from `$GITHUB_EVENT_PATH` alone.
GitHub Actions writes that file; Claude Code on the web does not — it
delivers the same `issues.labeled` trigger as `CCR_TRIGGER_*` environment
variables. So every CCR-run executor session missed its own trigger, hit
the no-payload exit, and took the documented fallback: list the ready
label, pick the oldest.

That fallback is the duplicate-execution bug reached from the other
direction. One scheduler run files every due dispatch seconds apart, so
every session that cannot name its trigger builds the *same* work list and
races the others over it; the claim in step 2 cannot save them because they
all read the list before any of them claims. Observed live 2026-07-28:
dispatch #772 selected by two sessions and claimed one second apart.

Two changes, both needed — teaching the shell the second transport removes
the cause, deleting the fallback removes the failure mode:

- `resolve-dispatch.mjs` now resolves its trigger from either transport.
  The Actions payload still answers in one shot. A CCR trigger names the
  issue but carries neither its labels nor its body, so it resolves in two:
  exit 13 `needs-issue` prints the number, the executor fetches that one
  issue over MCP, and re-invokes with `--issue-body-file` / `--issue-labels`
  for the identical validation. The shell still makes no GitHub call itself,
  so it stays runnable in the MCP-only, tokenless session.
- Exit 12 is now `no-trigger` and means STOP. No listing, no oldest-first,
  no selecting a dispatch at all. The scheduler's hourly re-arm is the
  recovery, in code, once.

Reading the issue's *current* labels on the CCR path is strictly better
than the event's `label.name`: a dispatch another session already claimed
no longer carries a ready label, so the lost race is caught at step 1
rather than at the claim.

Verified against the live trigger: the shell names #772 (was: exit 12),
and the second shot returns not-mine because #772 is already claimed.
7 new tests fail against the old shell; 787 pass, 0 blocking findings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012Cf25drJxNSwmRzjaJgpkQ
